   $q$According to the passage, why does a runner who starts too fast often slow down later?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The runner forgets to check the time at each half-mile marker.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-detail"},{"text":"The runner gets distracted by passing other runners near the start.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The runner builds up fatigue early and must slow down to recover.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The runner usually chooses a course with too many steep hills.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-cause-effect"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The runner builds up fatigue early and must slow down to recover.$q$, $q$Paragraph 2 says a runner who starts too fast "builds up fatigue early and must slow down to recover." Checking times is part of the training, not the cause of slowing down.$q$, 'draft', 'f8986b94-c83e-5075-9917-4d2b7cdc8c2d')
