-- Passage rg9-main-idea-summary-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('9d358747-3e82-5d3b-9a45-edf1c1c1ef15', 'rg9-main-idea-summary-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Saving the Pages of the Kessler Gazette$q$, $q$For ninety years, the fictional town of Kessler printed a weekly newspaper called the Kessler Gazette. It reported on school plays, harvest festivals, new bridges and the opening of every shop on Main Street. When the paper closed, its old issues were stored in the basement of the public library, stacked in boxes that few people ever opened.

By the time librarian Tomas Okonkwo-Reyes took a close look, the pages were in trouble. Old newspaper is made from cheap paper that turns yellow and brittle over time. Some pages cracked when they were turned. A leaking pipe had already ruined two years of issues. Okonkwo-Reyes realized that if nothing changed, much of the town's recorded history would simply crumble away.

His solution was to turn the pages into digital images. With a small grant, the library bought a scanner with a wide, flat bed that would not bend the fragile paper. Volunteers, including retirees and high school students, signed up for weekly shifts. They handled each page with clean cotton gloves, scanned it and saved it with the date and page number.

The project grew more useful than anyone expected. After the scans were finished, a computer program read the printed words so that people could search the whole collection by name or topic. Students used the archive for history projects. Families found wedding notices and sports scores from their grandparents' lives. A local museum used old advertisements to restore the original colors of a Main Street storefront.

The work took four years and more than eleven thousand pages. The original papers are now stored in acid-free boxes in a dry room, but most people never need to touch them. The town's history, once hidden in a damp basement, can now be read by anyone with a computer.$q$, 'draft')
