-- Passage rg9-main-idea-summary-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('4a7dc3ed-2ac8-55b2-ac58-946e023ef47a', 'rg9-main-idea-summary-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Why Bread Rises$q$, $q$A lump of bread dough can double in size while it sits on a kitchen counter. Nothing is added to it during that time, yet it grows soft and puffy. The change is the work of yeast, a tiny living fungus, and of a protein network that forms when flour and water are mixed.

Yeast feeds on sugars in the dough. Some of these sugars come from the flour itself, which contains starch that breaks down into simpler sugars when it gets wet. As the yeast feeds, it releases carbon dioxide gas. The gas forms tiny bubbles throughout the dough. On its own, however, the gas would simply escape into the air.

That is where the protein network comes in. When flour is mixed with water and kneaded, two proteins in the flour join to form gluten, a stretchy web of strands. Gluten acts like the rubber of a balloon. It stretches around each gas bubble and traps it inside, so the whole dough expands. Bakers knead dough for several minutes because the pushing and folding make the gluten stronger.

Temperature affects how quickly all of this happens. Yeast is most active in a warm place, which is why recipes often say to let dough rise somewhere cozy. If the dough is too cold, the yeast works slowly. If it gets too hot, the yeast dies. When the risen loaf goes into the oven, the heat makes the bubbles expand one last time, then sets the gluten and starch into a firm structure.

In a fictional study, students at Halvorsen Middle School baked loaves from the same recipe but let them rise at different temperatures. The loaves kept in a warm room rose highest, while the ones kept in a cool hallway stayed small and dense. The experiment showed that a good loaf depends on both living yeast and the conditions that let it work.$q$, 'draft')
