-- Passage rg9-main-idea-summary-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a7518606-4d24-520b-9ca8-67e6ff0874ed', 'rg9-main-idea-summary-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$How Salmon Find Their Way Home$q$, $q$Many salmon begin life in a small stream, swim downstream to the ocean and spend years traveling hundreds or even thousands of miles. Then, when they are ready to lay eggs, most of them return to the very stream where they hatched. For a long time, people wondered how a fish could find such a small place after such a long journey.

Scientists now believe that smell plays a central role. Every stream has its own mix of chemicals from the rocks, soil and plants along its banks. When young salmon are living in their home stream, they seem to memorize this scent. Years later, as adults near the coast, they follow that remembered smell upstream, choosing the right branch at each fork in the river.

Smell probably does not explain the ocean part of the journey, however. Far out at sea, a stream's scent is far too weak to follow. Researchers think salmon may use the Earth's magnetic field to find the general direction of their home coast. Once they reach the right river mouth, smell guides them the rest of the way.

In the fictional Copperline River, biologist Nadia Ferreira tested the smell idea. Her team raised young salmon in hatchery water mixed with a harmless chemical that has a distinct scent. Before the fish were released, a few drops of the same chemical were added to a small side stream. Years later, many of the returning adults swam into that side stream, even though they had never lived there.

Understanding this homing ability matters for the salmon's future. Dams and pollution can change a stream's water or block the path upstream. Ferreira's results suggest that protecting the natural scent of each stream may be as important as keeping the river open.$q$, 'draft')
