-- Passage rg9-main-idea-summary-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('14eb9536-e2f7-50e5-8785-84c442e117e4', 'rg9-main-idea-summary-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Every Student Should Learn to Speak Up$q$, $q$At Thornbury High School, students study algebra, chemistry and history, but most of them will graduate without ever practicing one of the most useful skills of all: speaking clearly in front of a group. I believe every high school, including ours, should require a semester course in public speaking.

The first reason is that nearly everyone will need this skill. Students give presentations in college classes. Adults explain ideas at job interviews, at work meetings and at neighborhood gatherings. Yet many people feel so nervous about speaking that they avoid it entirely. A course would give students a safe place to practice while the stakes are still low.

Second, public speaking can be taught. Some people assume that good speakers are simply born confident, but that is not the whole story. Speaking well involves skills that improve with practice, such as organizing ideas, making eye contact and slowing down when nerves take over. Last spring, our school's debate club ran a six-week workshop for thirty ninth graders. In a survey before the workshop, only nine said they felt comfortable speaking to a class. Afterward, twenty-two did.

Some people argue that the schedule is already too full and that students can learn to speak through regular class presentations. Those presentations help, but they are graded mostly on content, and students rarely receive advice on how they spoke. A dedicated course would focus on the speaking itself.

A required course would not turn every student into a star speaker, and it does not need to. Its goal would be to help each student feel ready to share an idea out loud. That readiness would serve them in every class they take and every job they hold. Thornbury should add the course to next year's schedule.$q$, 'draft')
