-- Passage rg9-story-details-inference-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('91abd822-3c50-58cf-b0f6-c568bc25b56f', 'rg9-story-details-inference-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Box Marked 1962$q$, $q$Amara Mensah had volunteered at the Fairhaven Historical Society for three weeks, and so far her work had been scanning old grocery receipts and town meeting notices that nobody would ever look up. Mrs. Okonjo, the director, said every page mattered. Amara nodded each time and privately counted the hours until she finished her twenty required service hours.

On Tuesday Mrs. Okonjo set a dented cardboard box on the scanning table. Someone had written 1962 on the lid in thick pencil. Inside were photographs of Delancey Street, the neighborhood that was torn down to build the highway interchange. Amara knew the name only because her grandfather, Kofi Mensah, mentioned it sometimes, usually while he was looking out the car window, and he never finished the sentence.

She scanned the first dozen photos quickly. Then she stopped. In one picture a boy of about ten stood in front of a shoe repair shop, holding a bicycle wheel above his head like a trophy. The sign over the door read Mensah and Sons.

Amara turned the photo over. On the back, in faded ink, someone had written: Kofi, after the race.

She did not scan the next photo for a long time. When Mrs. Okonjo walked past, Amara asked whether the society had a list of the families who had lived on Delancey Street. Mrs. Okonjo said no one had ever made one, because no one had ever asked.

That evening Amara stayed until the building closed, though her hours for the week were already complete. She had started a spreadsheet with three columns: name, address, and source. Only one row was filled in. Before she left, she printed a copy of the photograph, slid it into an envelope, and wrote a single word on the front: Grandpa.$q$, 'draft')
