-- Passage rg9-story-details-inference-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5022d12f-ce7f-5865-841f-2b29f7d165d3', 'rg9-story-details-inference-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Magpie Who Kept Score$q$, $q$In a hedgerow at the edge of a wheat field lived a magpie who was known for two things: her generosity and her memory. When the finch's nest blew down, she carried him three twigs. When the sparrows could not find water in August, she showed them the leak in the farmer's trough. Each time she helped, she said, "Remember, now," and she scratched a mark into the bark of her oak so that she would not forget either.

The wren who lived below her helped others too, though less grandly. She shared seeds, sang warnings when the hawk circled, and once spent an afternoon untangling a thrush from a loose piece of string. She made no marks on any tree. When the magpie asked how she kept track, the wren looked puzzled and said she had never thought to.

In November a wind came through the hedgerow and tore the magpie's nest from the oak. The magpie was not worried. She flew to her bark and read out her debts, and one by one the birds came to repay her. The finch brought three twigs, exactly. The sparrows brought one beakful of moss, since one trough was hardly worth more. Each bird paid precisely what was marked, bowed politely, and flew home.

By evening the magpie had half a nest and a cold wind at her back.

Then the wren arrived with a strand of wool. Behind her came the thrush, and behind the thrush came three young sparrows who owed the magpie nothing at all, since they had hatched only that spring. They worked until dark. Nobody counted the twigs.

The next morning the magpie looked at her finished nest for a long time. Then she flew down to the oak and, with her beak, began scratching the marks out of the bark.$q$, 'draft')
