-- Passage rg9-story-details-inference-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('38f58d7f-bce8-5256-98bf-e5eb274437e1', 'rg9-story-details-inference-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Bakery, Five A.M.$q$, $q$My father's hands know the dough before he does.
At five, while the street is still gray and empty,
he folds and presses, folds and presses,
and the radio plays low so it won't wake my sister.

He never measures the flour anymore.
He says the measuring cup is in his wrists.
When I tried it once, my loaves came out flat as books,
and he ate one anyway, with butter, smiling.

He came here with a suitcase and a recipe
his mother wrote on the back of a train ticket.
The ticket is taped inside the cabinet door,
the ink gone brown, the corners soft as cloth.
He does not read it. He touches it each morning
the way some people tap a doorframe for luck.

By six the first trays are out, and the window fogs,
and Mrs. Adeyemi from the laundromat knocks twice
for the round loaf she pays for on Fridays
and gets for free the other days of the week.
He tells her the free ones are mistakes.
They look exactly like the others.

Someday, he says, I will take over the ovens.
I do not know yet if I want to.
But this morning I came down at five without being asked,
and he moved over, just a little,
to make room for me at the table.$q$, 'draft')
