-- Passage rg9-text-structure-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('345b71a6-3d09-5934-a8ec-de1303c8b7b5', 'rg9-text-structure-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$How a Solar Oven Cooks$q$, $q$A solar oven can bake bread or cook a pot of rice using nothing but sunlight. It sounds like a magic trick, but the oven works by doing three simple jobs well: collecting light, turning that light into heat, and keeping the heat from escaping.

The first job belongs to the reflectors. These are flat panels covered in shiny foil, angled outward around the top of the oven like the petals of a flower. Sunlight that would otherwise miss the oven bounces off the panels and into the cooking box, so the box receives far more light than its own small opening could catch.

Next, the light must become heat. The inside of the box is painted flat black, and the cooking pot is usually dark as well. Dark surfaces absorb most of the light that strikes them and grow warm, much as a black shirt feels hotter than a white one on a summer afternoon.

Finally, the oven must hold on to that warmth. A clear glass or plastic lid lets sunlight pass in but slows the escape of warm air. The walls are packed with insulation, such as wool or folded cardboard, which keeps heat from leaking out through the sides. On a clear day, the inside of a well-built oven can reach about 150 degrees Celsius.

Because each job depends on the one before it, a weak link anywhere lowers the temperature. Students at Brightwater High School learned this when their first oven barely warmed a cup of water. The reflectors were fine, but they had left gaps in the insulation. After sealing the walls, their second test baked a tray of corn muffins in under two hours.$q$, 'draft')
