-- Passage rg9-theme-summary-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8a38b6e1-9414-5766-8c8d-d57008b44844', 'rg9-theme-summary-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Fox Who Borrowed Voices$q$, $q$In a valley ringed with pines lived a young fox named Sorrel who was ashamed of her own voice. It was a thin, sharp bark, like a stick snapping, and she thought it was the ugliest sound in the forest.

The owl's call, she decided, was much finer. So Sorrel practiced in the dark until she could make a low, round hoot. The owl heard her and blinked. "Very close," he said kindly. "But when a fox hoots, nobody knows what she means."

Next she tried the crow's harsh caw, and then the wolf's long howl. The crows laughed at her from the treetops. The wolves were not amused at all, since a howl means something particular to a wolf, and Sorrel's meant nothing.

By autumn Sorrel could imitate eleven different animals, and none of them listened to her. She had nearly forgotten her own bark.

One gray morning, after a night of heavy rain in the hills, a family of deer was grazing in the dry creek bed below her den. Sorrel looked up the valley and saw a wall of brown water rushing down between the rocks. There was no time to choose a borrowed voice. Out came the thin, sharp bark she had tried so hard to bury, and the deer bounded up the bank just before the water swept through.

Afterward an old doe climbed to where Sorrel stood and looked at her with surprise. "That sound," she said. "We have always known it. When a fox barks like that, something is wrong. We have been waiting all summer to hear it again."

Sorrel sat quietly for a long time. That winter she still listened to the owl and the crows, and sometimes she admired them. But when she spoke, she spoke as a fox.$q$, 'draft')
