-- Passage rg9-theme-summary-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c72ddb49-a2f2-590c-bd6d-77bc8f5c0dc1', 'rg9-theme-summary-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Station Six$q$, $q$On the last day of the field trip, Keoni Kahale's group collected rock samples from six stations along Miller Creek. Each sample went into a cloth bag with a paper tag listing the station number, the date, and the rock type the group thought it was. The bags would go to a county geologist, Dr. Fenwick, who was mapping the rock beneath the creek and had asked Mr. Osei's earth science class to help.

Keoni was in charge of tags. By station six it was nearly dark, the bus was waiting, and he wrote the last two tags in a hurry.

Three days later, while unpacking the extra samples the class had kept for its own shelf, he noticed something wrong. The stone tagged STATION 6 was pale and speckled, like the rock at station five. He had put the tags for stations five and six on the wrong bags, and he had done it the same way for both sets. The bags in Dr. Fenwick's lab were switched too.

Nobody else had noticed. The group's report had already earned an A. His teammate Rosa said the two samples looked nearly alike anyway, and that a county map would not change because of two bags.

Keoni thought about the map. Somewhere a line would be drawn in the wrong place because of his handwriting, and anyone who used it would trust that line.

He wrote an email to Mr. Osei that evening. He rewrote it four times, deleting every sentence that sounded like an excuse.

Mr. Osei forwarded it to Dr. Fenwick, who wrote back the next morning. She had noticed that station six looked odd and had planned to throw out both samples. Now she could keep them. "Every map has errors," she wrote. "The useful ones are the errors someone reports."

At the end of her email, she asked whether Keoni would help label the next batch.$q$, 'draft')
