-- Passage rg9-word-parts-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1156a920-29a1-553f-9c4c-fa2047002925', 'rg9-word-parts-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Night Flights Over the City$q$, $q$Every spring and fall, millions of migratory birds pass over cities while most people are asleep. Warblers, thrushes and sparrows travel thousands of kilometers between their winter homes and their summer nesting grounds, and many of them stop in city parks along the way to rest and eat.

Most of these small birds are nocturnal travelers. They fly in the dark, when the air is cooler and fewer hawks are hunting, and they spend the daylight hours feeding. On a quiet night, a patient listener standing on a rooftop can sometimes hear their short flight calls, thin chirps that are barely audible over the hum of traffic below.

Scientists still debate exactly how the birds find their way. Young birds seem to be born knowing which direction to fly, and older birds also use the stars and the setting sun. Many researchers believe the birds can sense the planet's geomagnetic field as well, using the invisible pull of the earth's magnetism like a built-in compass.

Cities create a serious problem for these travelers. Bright light emitted by office towers and stadiums can confuse birds flying overhead, drawing them down toward the glowing buildings, where they may circle until they are exhausted or strike a window.

The good news is that this problem has a simple fix. In several cities, building managers now dim or switch off their lights during the busiest weeks of migration. Volunteers walk the downtown sidewalks at dawn to count injured birds, and their records show that darker skylines lead to far fewer collisions. A single switch, it turns out, can help a bird finish a journey of thousands of kilometers.$q$, 'draft')
