insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('193e2fdc-de11-51d1-8b2b-3452ea4c1883', 'rg9-word-parts-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Translation Table$q$, $q$On Thursday afternoons, the public library set up what the staff called the translation table. Neighbors brought letters, forms and instructions they could not read, and volunteers helped them understand. Mei-Lin Zhou had signed up because she was bilingual, fluent in both English and Mandarin, and her counselor had said the hours would count toward her service requirement.

Her first visitor was Mr. Haddad, an older man holding a notice from his building manager. He spoke Arabic and some Mandarin from years of working in a restaurant kitchen, but very little English. Mei-Lin read the notice aloud in Mandarin, slowly, and Mr. Haddad nodded along until she reached the part about the water being shut off. "Tuesday?" he interjected, before she could finish the sentence. "The whole day?"

Mei-Lin checked. It was only Tuesday morning. She transcribed the important details onto an index card, writing the date and the hours in large, clear numbers so he could keep the card on his refrigerator.

At the next seat, a volunteer named Tomas was helping a family fill out a school form. Tomas was multilingual; he moved easily among Spanish, Portuguese and English, switching languages in the middle of a sentence without seeming to notice.

Before Mr. Haddad left, he asked Mei-Lin to write one more thing. She thought for a moment, then added a postscript at the bottom of the card, below the date and the hours: Fill a pitcher on Monday night. He laughed, tapped the card twice and tucked it into his coat pocket.

That evening, Mei-Lin realized she had forgotten to log her hours. She did not mind. She was already planning to come back next Thursday.$q$, 'draft')
