-- Passage rg9-word-parts-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8322960e-ac2d-515a-aa45-ae32e3bdf95f', 'rg9-word-parts-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Drawing a New Bus Route$q$, $q$When the town of Riverside Falls decided to add a bus route, the planners did not start with a map. They started with questions. Who needed to get somewhere, at what time, and why could they not get there now?

For years, buses had run only through the old downtown. Meanwhile, most new homes had been built in suburban neighborhoods on the edge of town, where streets curved into cul-de-sacs and the nearest grocery store was miles away. Families there without a car had few choices.

The planners began by studying demographic data: the ages, incomes and household sizes of the people living in each neighborhood. They noticed that one area had many older residents who no longer drove and another had many teenagers who needed rides to the high school and to after-school jobs.

Next, they surveyed riders. Hundreds of people described their daily trips, and the answers pointed again and again to one spot: the junction where Route 9 meets Mill Road, near the hospital, the community college and two large employers.

The final plan places a new transit center at that intersection. Buses from the suburbs will arrive there every twenty minutes, and riders transferring to the downtown line will be able to walk a few steps to the next bus instead of waiting half an hour. A covered shelter, benches and a screen showing arrival times are included in the design.

The planners admit the route will need adjustments once real riders start using it. Still, they believe that listening first, and drawing lines second, gave them a better plan than any map alone could have.$q$, 'draft')
