-- Passage rg9-word-parts-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d9f9c526-c701-5695-880f-42fe4452c2d2', 'rg9-word-parts-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Knot Room$q$, $q$Marisol Quispe spent her summer volunteering at the harbor's maritime museum, where old fishing boats, ship models and brass instruments filled three floors of a former warehouse. Her favorite space was a small room on the top floor that the staff called the knot room.

The room had been vacant for years, used only to store broken display cases. Then the museum's director, Mr. Okonkwo, decided to turn it into a workshop where visitors could learn to tie the knots that sailors once used every day. He asked Marisol to help.

On the first morning, Mr. Okonkwo showed her a coil of thick rope from a cargo ship that had been sunk in the harbor long ago. The rope had stayed submerged in cold, muddy water for decades before divers brought it up. It had been manufactured by hand, he explained, twisted strand by strand in a long building called a ropewalk, before machines took over the work.

Marisol practiced until her fingers ached. She learned the bowline, which makes a loop that will not slip, and the clove hitch, used to tie a line to a post. Mr. Okonkwo told her that sailors needed these knots to navigate safely, steering their ships through storms and tying them securely in strange ports.

By August, Marisol was teaching the knots herself. One afternoon, a young boy struggled with the bowline for ten minutes before finally getting it right. He held it up and beamed at her. Marisol felt deeply gratified, more pleased by that small loop of rope than by anything else she had done all summer.$q$, 'draft')
