-- Passage rg10-argument-evidence-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f414b75c-54a2-5952-b620-2f28e72e1228', 'rg10-argument-evidence-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Keep Handwriting on the Schedule$q$, $q$The Ashgrove school district is considering a plan to stop teaching cursive in its elementary schools and use that time for keyboarding instead. As a parent of two young children, I urge the school board to reject this plan.

First, consider the research. A study at the Lindqvist Institute compared college students who took lecture notes by hand with students who typed them on laptops. A week later, the students who wrote by hand remembered more of the lecture's main ideas. Clearly, cursive is essential to learning.

Second, handwriting connects us to our families. My grandmother wrote me a letter every month when I was a child, and I still treasure every one. My children deserve to read the letters their own grandparents send them, and some of those letters are written in cursive.

Third, we must think about where this decision leads. If we stop teaching cursive, children will soon forget how to sign their names. Then they will not be able to sign forms, open bank accounts or apply for jobs. Before long, we will have a generation that cannot manage the simplest adult tasks.

Supporters of the plan say keyboarding is a skill students will use every day. That may be true, but the choice in front of the board is simple. Either we teach handwriting, or we accept children who cannot think for themselves.

Cursive takes only twenty minutes a day in second and third grade. That is a small price to pay for a skill that has served people for centuries.$q$, 'draft')
