-- Passage rg10-argument-evidence-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('16e721c3-5d8f-5ac5-8193-fc0cb43fc4ed', 'rg10-argument-evidence-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Do Thin Shoes Prevent Injuries?$q$, $q$Last spring, Coach Marcus Adeyemi of the Tallis River Running Club made a bold announcement. Thin-soled running shoes, he said, had cut his runners' injuries by two thirds. His evidence came from his own club. In the season before the switch, his twenty runners reported nine injuries. After every runner moved to thin-soled shoes, the club reported only three.

The numbers are striking, and the coach's enthusiasm is understandable. Still, a careful reader should ask whether they show what he says they show.

The first problem is that the shoes were not the only thing that changed. In the same season, the club adopted a new training plan that lowered weekly mileage by about a fifth and added two rest days each month. Any drop in injuries might come from the lighter schedule rather than from the footwear, and the club's records cannot separate the two.

The second problem is the lack of a comparison group. No runners in the club kept their old shoes, so there is no way to see what would have happened without the change. A season with fewer injuries can also happen by chance, especially in a group of only twenty people.

The third problem is quieter. Four runners left the club early in the season, and the coach did not count them in his totals. Club members say at least two of them quit because their feet and calves ached in the new shoes. If those runners had stayed and been injured, the final count would look quite different.

None of this proves that thin shoes are harmful or useless. It means only that one club's season cannot settle the question. A fair test would assign some runners to each type of shoe, keep their training the same and track everyone who starts.$q$, 'draft')
