   $q$How do the four runners who left the club affect the reliability of the coach's numbers?$q$, $q$[{"text":"They make the numbers more reliable, since only committed runners were counted.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reversed-reasoning"},{"text":"They prove that thin shoes caused at least four injuries that season.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overstates-evidence"},{"text":"They may hide problems with the shoes, since some left because of aches.","is_correct":true},{"text":"They have no effect, since the coach counted twenty runners in both seasons.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$They may hide problems with the shoes, since some left because of aches.$q$, $q$The article says at least two left "because their feet and calves ached in the new shoes," and they were left out of the totals, so the low count may look better than reality. Their leaving does not prove four injuries; it only means the count may be incomplete.$q$, 'draft', '16e721c3-5d8f-5ac5-8193-fc0cb43fc4ed')
