-- Passage rg10-argument-evidence-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b116a008-5aaa-5731-ac44-593527db3f0b', 'rg10-argument-evidence-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$What Really Emptied the Calder Canal$q$, $q$For most of the nineteenth century, the Calder Canal carried coal, grain and lumber 140 miles from the hill country to the port of Selbridge. By 1880 it was nearly empty, and its locks were crumbling. Local histories often blame the great drought of 1871, which dried the canal's upper sections. Historian Lena Szabo argues that this explanation is mistaken. In her view, competition from the railroad, not the drought, ended the canal's working life.

Szabo's case begins with the canal company's own ledgers. Cargo on the canal peaked in 1857 at about 410,000 tons. In 1858 the Selbridge and Western Railroad opened a line along nearly the same route, and canal cargo began to fall that very year. By 1870, the year before the drought, the canal carried only 160,000 tons, well under half its peak.

She then looks closely at the drought itself. Low water closed the upper canal for about two months in the summer of 1871. When autumn rains refilled it, boats could travel the full length again. Yet cargo did not return to its earlier level. In 1872 the canal carried even less than it had in 1870.

Finally, Szabo points to the shippers. Letters from grain merchants in the 1860s complain that canal boats took six days to reach Selbridge, while trains made the trip in less than one. Several merchants wrote that they had moved all their freight to the railroad.

Szabo does not claim the drought was unimportant to the families who worked the canal that summer. She argues only that it struck a business already in steep decline. The ledgers, she concludes, tell the story clearly: the canal was losing its customers long before it lost its water.$q$, 'draft')
