-- Passage rg10-argument-evidence-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('3fc0297b-639a-5273-b2e8-b90e3fb4a837', 'rg10-argument-evidence-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Let Visitors Take Pictures$q$, $q$For thirty years, the Ostrander Museum of Art has banned all photography in its galleries. The museum should lift this ban and allow visitors to take pictures without flash.

The museum's main reason for the ban is that camera flashes can fade old paintings. That concern deserves a serious answer, and there is one. Nearly every phone camera can turn off its flash. In addition, a study by the Brennick Conservation Laboratory measured the light that paintings receive and found that a single flash delivers far less light than one hour under ordinary gallery lamps. Even so, a no-flash rule would remove the risk entirely.

Photography would also bring in more visitors. Teenagers love sharing pictures, and my niece told me she would visit far more often if she could photograph her favorite paintings. Opening the galleries to cameras would surely fill them with young people.

The museum's leaders apparently believe that every visitor with a phone is a vandal waiting for a chance to damage the collection. That attitude insults the thousands of people who come to the Ostrander because they love art.

Pictures also help people learn. An art student who photographs a painting can study its brushwork at home, compare it with other works and return with new questions. A ban treats curiosity as a threat.

Many museums in other cities already allow photography without flash, and their collections remain in good condition. The Ostrander should trust its visitors in the same way.$q$, 'draft')
