-- Passage rg10-argument-evidence-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('4347f33f-951e-5cbd-95ac-5e1953240d65', 'rg10-argument-evidence-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Stop Feeding Bread at Millbrook Pond$q$, $q$On any warm weekend, families gather at the edge of Millbrook Pond and toss bread to the ducks and geese. It looks harmless, even charming. As a volunteer who has helped care for the pond for six years, I believe the park board should post signs asking visitors not to feed bread to the birds.

Bread fills birds up without giving them what they need. It is low in the protein and minerals that waterfowl get from plants, insects and seeds. Young birds that eat mostly bread can develop weak wing bones, a condition the county wildlife office has recorded in several goslings at Millbrook over the past two years.

Uneaten bread is a second problem. Each summer, our volunteer group tests the water at six spots around the pond. Near the two picnic areas where feeding is heaviest, we have repeatedly found higher nutrient levels and thicker green algae than at the four quieter spots. Soggy bread that sinks and rots adds nutrients to the water, and those nutrients feed the algae.

The number of geese has also climbed, from about 40 five years ago to more than 200 today. With so many birds crowded together, they must be getting sick more often, and the bread is clearly to blame for the crowding.

I understand that feeding ducks is a cherished family tradition, and I do not want to end it. Visitors could instead offer small amounts of birdseed, cracked corn or thawed peas, which the park office could sell for a dollar a bag.

A few simple signs could protect the pond and the birds while keeping the joy of a family visit.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
