-- Passage rg10-authors-purpose-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f863236c-afb5-59f4-b253-59d685a12316', 'rg10-authors-purpose-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Stay for Year Three$q$, $q$From an editorial by Tobias Mensah, a junior, in the Westbrook High School student newspaper.

Last spring, when it came time to choose electives, most of my friends dropped their world language class. They had finished the two years required for graduation, and they wanted room for something new. I understood. I almost did the same thing. I am glad I didn't, and I think more of you should stay too.

Here is the honest truth about the first two years of a language: they are mostly preparation. You memorize verb endings, practice ordering food in a pretend restaurant, and learn the words for classroom objects. It can feel like you are collecting tools without ever building anything. Third year is when you finally start to build. This fall, our class read a short novel, argued about its ending, and wrote letters to students at a partner school overseas. For the first time, I was using the language to say something I actually meant.

Some people will say that translation apps make language classes pointless. Why spend years learning what a phone can do in seconds? It is a fair question. But an app can give you words without giving you understanding. It cannot tell you why a joke is funny, why a certain phrase is polite in one country and rude in another, or what it feels like when a stranger's face lights up because you made the effort to speak their language.

Quitting after two years is like training for a race and stopping just before the starting line. You have done the hard, repetitive part. The reward is right ahead of you.

So before you fill out your schedule, think about what you have already invested. Then ask yourself whether you want to leave just as it starts to pay off.$q$, 'draft')
