insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('70a8f531-992f-5c7d-9e65-b26e8767e4a4', 'rg10-character-setting-plot-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Heron and the Kingfisher$q$, $q$Where the river widened into a still brown pond, a grey heron had fished alone for many seasons. The heron was proud of one thing above all: its patience. It could stand on one leg from sunrise to noon without moving a feather, and when a fish finally drifted near, the heron's beak struck once and never missed.

In spring, a kingfisher moved into the bank across the water. It fished the opposite way, diving from a branch a dozen times an hour, splashing, missing, diving again, and chattering the whole time. The heron found it ridiculous. Privately, the heron also counted the kingfisher's catches, and some days they were more than its own.

That summer no rain fell. The pond shrank to a muddy ring, and the fish grew few and wary. The heron stood longer and caught less. The kingfisher, who flew farther each day, returned one morning and landed on a reed near the heron, closer than it had ever dared.

"Upstream, past the fallen willow, there is a pool the sun cannot reach," it said. "It is deep and full of fish. I have been fishing it alone, and it is too quiet. Come with me."

The heron did not move. To follow a bird who could not keep still would be to admit that stillness was not enough. "I have always fished here," it said.

The kingfisher flew off without argument. For three more days the heron stood in the shrinking pond, catching nothing, its pride growing thinner along with its body. On the fourth morning it lifted its wide wings and flew upstream, slowly, past the fallen willow.

The kingfisher was waiting on a branch above the dark pool. It did not mention the three days. It simply moved over to make room.

Moral: Pride that refuses help will soon have nothing left to be proud of.$q$, 'draft')
