-- Passage rg10-comparing-texts-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('124c130c-f65e-56a3-973e-67d42d69b076', 'rg10-comparing-texts-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Waiting for the Right Moment$q$, $q$Text 1: The Heron Who Waited (an invented fable)

On the bank of a slow brown river lived a heron who believed that every task deserved a perfect moment. When the other birds fished at dawn, the heron said the water was too cloudy. At noon, the sun glared too brightly on the surface. At dusk, the wind ruffled the shallows and hid the fish. Each day the heron stood on one leg and waited for the river to become exactly right.

The kingfisher, who caught small fish in every kind of weather, offered advice. "The river will never hold still for you," she said. "You must learn to strike through ripples." The heron only lifted its beak higher. By the end of summer the heron had grown so thin that its feathers hung loose, and the river, which had never once been perfect, flowed on without noticing. Those who wait for a flawless hour may wait forever.

Text 2: Golden Hour (a story that retells the fable)

Mireille Okonkwo had three days left to enter the county photography contest, and she had not taken a single picture she liked. The theme was "Water in Motion." Every morning she biked to the dam spillway with her secondhand camera, and every morning something was wrong: a gray sky, a jogger in the frame, a gust that blew spray onto her lens.

On the second evening her younger brother, Tobi, tagged along, skipping stones while she frowned at the clouds. "You keep saying the light isn't right," he said. "Maybe the light is just the light." Mireille started to answer, then stopped. One of his stones had just skipped four times, leaving a chain of rings across the gold-streaked water.

She raised the camera and pressed the shutter before the rings faded. The picture was slightly tilted, and a dark smudge of cloud crossed one corner. She submitted it anyway, the night before the deadline. It did not win first place. It earned an honorable mention, and the judge wrote on the back of the card that the photo felt "caught, not posed." Mireille pinned the card above her desk. She still liked a clean sky, but she no longer waited for one.$q$, 'draft')
