insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ef91595a-cd70-58eb-906b-5331fee2fab5', 'rg10-comparing-texts-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Leaving Room$q$, $q$Text 1: The Singer Who Lost Her Echo (an invented legend)

High in an invented mountain valley, the legend goes, lived a singer named Oriel whose voice was the loudest in the land. When villagers sang together at the harvest, Oriel sang over all of them, and the cliffs sent her voice back so strongly that no one else could be heard. The other singers grew quiet and finally stopped coming.

One morning Oriel sang to the cliffs and nothing came back. Her echo had left. She sang louder, then louder still, and the silence only deepened. At last, tired, she sat by the river and listened to the water, the birds and a shepherd humming far away. She hummed softly along with him. From the cliffs, faint and small, her echo returned, joined to his. The valley says that the echo comes only to those who leave room for it.

Text 2: Monitor Mix (a story told by a bass player)

Our school jazz combo had five members and one problem: Caspian Reyes played trumpet louder than the rest of us put together. At our first concert, a spring fundraiser at the library, his solos were bright and fearless, but during the group sections he blasted right over the piano and buried the vibraphone completely. I play bass, so I am used to not being noticed, but even I could tell the audience was hearing a trumpet with some background noise.

Our director, Ms. Albescu, did not lecture him. Before the next rehearsal she turned the small speaker that let Caspian hear himself, called a monitor, so that it faced away from him. "Play the tune," she said. "If you can't hear yourself, listen to us."

Caspian looked annoyed for the first eight bars. Then something changed. He started leaning toward the piano, matching its phrasing, dropping his volume in the soft passages so he could catch Nadia's vibraphone lines. When he finally took his solo, it sounded bigger than before, maybe because we were all underneath it, holding it up.

After rehearsal he asked Ms. Albescu to keep the monitor turned around for our next concert at the senior center. I do not think he ever said he had been too loud. He just played as if he had discovered that the rest of us were there.$q$, 'draft')
